cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "blinkrate: measurement rules and reported electric distribution reliability"
message: "If you use these findings or this code, please cite it."
type: software
authors:
  - given-names: Andrew
version: 1.0.0
date-released: 2026-09-09
license: MIT
keywords:
  - SAIDI
  - IEEE 1366
  - major event day
  - electric distribution reliability
  - EIA-861
abstract: >-
  IEEE 1366 permits utilities to exclude "major event days" from reported SAIDI
  using a threshold set 2.5 standard deviations up a log-normal fit to their own
  daily outage history. Using 6,245 utility-state-years of EIA-861 reliability
  filings from 2013-2018 and 2022, this work inverts the closed-form exclusion
  identity r = Phi(beta - sigma) to show that re-evaluating the same filed
  outage records at other threshold values moves customer-weighted fleet SAIDI
  over a 7.66x range; that 29.8% of filings report no major event days at all
  (12.0x excess mass over a polynomial counterfactual); that between 40 and 57%
  of large investor-owned utilities change performance quartile depending on
  which of the two published series is used to rank them; and that a
  within-utility design covering 53 advanced-metering rollouts finds no
  detectable reliability effect in a design powered to detect 24%. A geographic
  placebo separates weather-driven spikes in the excluded portion from a slower
  methodological drift into using the exclusion at all. The threshold
  sensitivity is model-implied and inherits IEEE 1366's own log-normality
  assumption; an adversarial track shows its direction survives heavier-tailed
  daily outage distributions while its magnitude does not.
